- op: discuss  # 1d93
  at: '2026-06-02T05:47:41Z'  # 1d93
  by: agent  # 1d93
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1d93
  clock: 6  # 1d93
  nonce: 1d93  # 1d93
  message: "Pass-26 provenance-chain probe — INV-dulah scope expansion from 12 to 1663:\n\nF26.11: node.id last segment ≠ node.kind on 1663 of 34253 nodes (4.86%). All 1663 are external_symbol kind with id-suffix \"unresolved\" (e.g., `python:pathlib:0-0:Path:unresolved` where kind=external_symbol). Documented format spec.md line 1769 declares `lang:path:span:name:kind` as the node.id shape; external_symbol nodes violate this universally by using \"unresolved\" instead of \"external_symbol\" in the last segment.\n\nThis is the same root mechanism as INV-dulah's original 12-node finding (different code paths format the id without normalization), but the scope is much larger: 1663 nodes vs 12. The \"unresolved\" suffix is the dep-resolution fallback used by the orchestrator when an external reference can't be resolved to a concrete kind. It's documented behavior at the producer side but violates the consumer-side format contract.\n\nSub-classification of INV-dulah's umbrella (from existing root cause + pass-26 expansion):\n- ~1663 external_symbol nodes with id-suffix \"unresolved\" (pass-26 finding; new dominant population)\n- 7 Python route IDs with 5 colons (route path contains literal `:` separator — INV-dulah existing)\n- 2 npm packages with 3 colons (missing span segment — INV-dulah existing)\n- 2 Rust nodes with 6/8 colons (`::` namespacing — INV-dulah existing)\n- 1 Solidity \"Not owner\" carry-over (INV-dulah existing)\n\nTotal: ~1675 nodes with id-format violations (4.89% of substrate). The \"1663 unresolved\" group dominates; the original 12 are now a tiny sub-tail of the broader format-contract gap. INV-dulah's statement could update from \"12 nodes\" to \"1663+12=1675 nodes\" and the root cause from \"different code paths format node.id without escaping\" to \"the documented `kind` suffix slot carries non-kind values in 2 distinct cases: 'unresolved' for dep-resolution fallback, and literal kind names for other paths.\"\n\nA spec-compliance question for human review: should the `kind` slot accept ONLY values from the canonical kind catalog (then \"unresolved\" is a violation), or accept any string (then external_symbol's \"unresolved\" suffix is acceptable and the format spec needs updating to say \"suffix string indicating discovery state\")? Either interpretation yields a concrete fix; the current state is ambiguous."  # 1d93
- op: discuss  # 0e61
  at: '2026-06-02T06:13:09Z'  # 0e61
  by: agent  # 0e61
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0e61
  clock: 7  # 0e61
  nonce: '0e61'  # 0e61
  message: "Pass-27 INV-dulah further scope expansion — name-slot mismatch (1156 additional nodes, distinct from pass-26 F26.11's kind-slot cohort).\n\nPass-26 F26.11 expanded INV-dulah from 12 to 1675 nodes by counting the 1663 `external_symbol` nodes whose id has \"unresolved\" in the `kind` slot (`python:pathlib:0-0:Path:unresolved`). Pass-27's round-trip parse probe finds an ADDITIONAL 1156 nodes — disjoint from F26.11's cohort — where the `name` slot of node.id does not equal node.name. Total cumulative INV-dulah scope now ~2831 unique nodes violating the documented `lang:path:span:name:kind` round-trip.\n\nThe 1156 name-slot mismatches split into three sub-patterns:\n\n(a) **Bash file-kind literal \"file\" placeholder** — 901 file-kind nodes from the bash analyzer:\n    id=`bash:.githooks/commit-msg:1-1:file:file`, name=`.githooks/commit-msg`\n    The id construction uses the literal string \"file\" in the name slot instead of the basename. Affects every parsed bash file (matches every file-kind Symbol from `find_bash_files` output).\n\n(b) **Linker call_site placeholder name** — 130 call_site nodes from http-linker / database-query-linker:\n    id=`python:.../test_http_linker.py:229-229:http_client:call_site`, name=`DELETE /api/users/1`\n    id=`python:.../cache.py:215-215:db_query:call_site`, name=`DELETE items`\n    Linker constructs call_site id with a generic per-linker token (`http_client`, `db_query`) in the name slot; the actual call detail (HTTP verb + path, SQL verb + table) lives in node.name. Round-trip parser sees the linker token, not the call detail.\n\n(c) **WebSocket-linker normalized route in name slot** — ~124 function-kind nodes:\n    id=`python:.../serve.py:433-433:/ws-endpoint:function`, name=`ws:/ws`\n    The websocket-linker writes a normalized route path (`/ws-endpoint`) into the name slot, but stores the original route URL (`ws:/ws`) in node.name — and the original URL contains a literal `:` that would have broken parsing anyway. Two writer bugs compound: wrong-field-in-slot + unescaped-colon-in-name.\n\nCommon pattern: the writers chose a fixed string (placeholder, linker-token, or normalized form) for the id slot but stored a different value as node.name. INV-dulah's statement captures this generally (\"Different code paths format node.id without escaping or normalizing colons in the embedded path/name fields\"), but the historical scope-12 examples are all colon-escape problems. The new ~2831-node cumulative scope is dominated by **wrong-value-in-slot** rather than **unescaped-colon-in-value** — different mechanism, same external symptom (id doesn't round-trip).\n\nRecommended fix path candidate (not a binding scope change, just observation):\n1. ADR-0034 already governs id-construction discipline. The 4 distinct writers (bash file, http-linker call_site, database-query-linker call_site, websocket-linker function) each bypass the canonical factory.\n2. The round-trip-must-equal invariant could be added to the id-format validator class (currently checks colon count, doesn't verify each slot equals the corresponding node field).\n\nProbe artifact: /tmp/pass27_probe2.py + /tmp/pass27_probe4.py / outputs at /tmp/pass27_output[2,4].log.\n\nNo status change; pass-27 just enriches the cohort breakdown beyond F26.11."  # 0e61
